hole group and the result expanded again, so the leftmost group varies slowest.\n\nMatching splits both the pattern and the path on `/`, skipping an escaped `/`, and compares segment by segment; a pattern segment that is exactly `**` instead matches zero or more whole path segments, none of which may begin with `.`. Any other segment must match its path segment as a whole. Within a segment, `\\` makes the next character literal, `?` matches exactly one character, and any run of `*` matches zero or more characters. A bracket class opens with `[`, is negated by a leading `!` or `^`, holds single characters and `from-to` code-point ranges, treats a `]` written first as a literal, treats a `-` that has no character after it as a literal, honours a backslash inside itself, and closes at the first `]` that is neither first nor escaped. An extended group is one of `?`, `*`, `+`, `@` or `!` immediately followed by `(`, holding `|`-separated sub-patterns and closing at its matching `)`; sub-patterns may themselves hold groups. `?(…)` matches zero or one of its alternatives, `*(…)` zero or more, `+(…)` one or more, `@(…)` exactly one, and `!(…)` matches any run of characters that is not one of them. All five may be followed by more pattern, and the segment matches when some division of the text satisfies the whole segment.\n\nA path segment that begins with `.` matches only a pattern segment whose first element is a literal `.`, written plainly or escaped; a wildcard, a class or a group at the start of a segment never matches that dot. Neither `?`, nor `*`, nor a class, nor a group ever matches a `/`, because matching is done inside one segment.\n\nA failure writes one line to standard error and exits 2, having written nothing to standard output. The messages are `error: usage: cli.js expand|match` when the arguments are not exactly one of the two mode words; `error: expand needs exactly one pattern line` when `expand` receives any number of lines other than one; `error: matc